      "id": "idea",
      "narration": "No one is crazy with money, Housel says, even when they look it. Think about how differently two people can see the same world. Someone who grew up in the Great Depression learned that the stock market is a casino that eats your savings, so they hoard cash for the rest of their life. Someone born in nineteen seventy came of age when stocks soared, so they pour everything in without blinking. Same country, opposite lessons, and both feel obviously correct, because each person is reacting to the tiny slice of history they personally lived through. Neither of them studied a chart and chose wrong. They simply trusted what their own life had burned into them, which is exactly what you and I quietly do too.",
